From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 23:13:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: rkvdec: decode one stream on both RK3588 cores with
 the CCU

The RK3588 has two VDPU381 cores and a core coordination unit (CCU)
between them. In the Rockchip BSP, the cores work in "CCU" mode: every
frame goes to whichever core is idle, so consecutive frames of one
stream are decoded at the same time, and the CCU makes a core that
reads reference pixels or co-located motion vectors from a frame
another core is still decoding wait until they are written. The frames
of a stream are told apart from other streams by a film index in the
multi-core control register (reg028). Userspace programs nothing
special for it: the BSP kernel driver connects the cores to the CCU and
sets the film index.

Support the CCU, described by a rockchip,rk3588-vdec-ccu node that the
cores refer to with rockchip,ccu:

 - Connect every core to the CCU before a job (work mode bits in the
   link block, core enable in the CCU) and tell the CCU when the core
   starts.
 - Give every context a film index, and don't let a core reset itself
   when a reference read waits for the other core.
 - Keep all the cores and the CCU powered while one of the cores works,
   as the BSP does.
 - For H.264 and HEVC, give contexts one job slot per core and let
   job_ready() start the next frame of a stream while the previous one
   is still being decoded, unless the CAPTURE buffer is held for a
   second field, or the frames being decoded still read from the next
   CAPTURE buffer: userspace may queue a buffer again as soon as no
   future frame predicts from it.

VP9 stays serial within a stream: rkvdec adapts the probabilities of
the next frame in software from the symbol counts of the previous one.
Without a CCU node, nothing changes.

The CCU register layout and the programming sequence come from the
Rockchip BSP kernel, drivers/video/rockchip/mpp/mpp_rkvdec2_link.c
(soft CCU mode) and the MPP userspace register definitions
(mpp/hal/rkdec/vdpu34x_com.h, SWREG28_MULTIPLY_CORE_CTRL).

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
